From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 21:34:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 85/85] fix(polecat): refuse a claim onto a molecule a live
 session still holds (gcp-mjjg)

A `mol-polecat-work` implement step can be returned to the pool while the
session working it is alive and mid-task, and until now nothing refused the
duplicate that followed. On 2026-09-07 winnow-iaroy was released twice --
20:11:56.506Z and 20:33:37.970Z -- while gastown__polecat-gc-8a4d held the
molecule and was state=active, mid-sweep over multi-minute retrieval
evaluations. The pool then spawned gastown__polecat-gc-psfm, whose hook claim
succeeded cleanly at 20:37:51Z. Work survived only because that second polecat
recognised the race and backed off; had it implemented, it would have stomped a
live worktree or corrupted a concurrent history.jsonl write.

The releaser is gc's NativeDoltStore.ReleaseIfCurrent: both releases are Dolt
commits carrying its literal transaction message, `gc: release bead
winnow-iaroy if current`, and it is the only path in gascity@aff678389 that
emits that string. It swaps status/assignee inside one transaction and writes
no bd `events` row, so the release is invisible to every audit surface a
witness reads -- which is exactly why the incident report could not name it.
The hypothesised culprit, this prompt's own claim-verify release, is ruled out:
that path writes via `gc bd update`, which commits as `bd: update` under the
polecat's actor and does record an event. Neither release commit has either
property. Full evidence is on the bead; the gc-side defect is filed separately,
since it cannot be fixed from this pack.

What makes a pack-side guard possible is the shape of the blast radius. The
release only ever reaches the STEP bead: the work bead carries no
`gc.routed_to` and so is never a release candidate, and the sibling steps were
dependency-blocked and never entered a ready snapshot. The work bead's assignee
therefore survives as the one durable record of who owns the molecule. The
guard walks step -> molecule root -> convoy -> work bead and consults it before
any work begins, declining when a DIFFERENT session holds it and that session
is still live. A dead prior owner is the ordinary post-restart resume (pool
restarts mint a new identity) and must not stall the engine, so only liveness
blocks -- and unreadable liveness declines too, because the two mistakes are
not symmetric: declining costs one pool slot, proceeding stomps a live
worktree. A declining polecat restores the step to its owner, nudges the
witness, and drains without touching code, branch, or worktree.

Also fixes the read-success test the incident report flagged independently:
SHOW_OK required a non-empty assignee, but `gc bd show` omits the field
entirely when null, so "read fine, genuinely unassigned" and "read failed" were
one observation and a readable unassigned bead took the CLAIM_RELEASED path
instead of CLAIM_REJECTED. It now keys on the bead echoing its own id, and the
retry budget goes from 3 tries at 1s to 5 with a rising backoff -- `gc` pays
seconds of startup before reaching Dolt, and the box was at load 44.91.

Validation: test_polecat_live_owner_guard.sh extracts the guard verbatim from
the prompt and runs it against a stubbed gc over seven cases, including the
incident replayed with its real bead and session ids. Verified RED by
neutering the liveness decision (the live-owner case then falls through to the
work) before confirming GREEN. Full pack suite, `go vet`, `go build`,
`go test ./...`, validate_registry.py --require-git, and the Python pack suite
all pass.

Follow-up: the gascity-side defect -- ReleaseIfCurrent reaching a bead whose
owner is demonstrably alive, and doing so without an audit event -- is filed
against gascity and cross-referenced from gcp-544r, gcp-cvbs and gcp-xomt,
which share the shape but have distinct triggers.
